    content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Workers' compensation cases arrive on a different timeline and through different search behavior than most other personal injury matters, which means a marketing plan copied wholesale from a general accident practice rarely performs as well as one built specifically around how injured workers actually search for help. Someone hurt on the job is frequently dealing with an employer, an insurance adjuster, or a company doctor telling them their claim is straightforward, and searches online precisely because something about that story doesn't add up. Reaching that person requires a mix of channels working together rather than any single tactic carrying the entire campaign." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Start With Local SEO and Map Listings" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A well-optimized Google Business Profile, complete with accurate practice area categories, consistent contact information, and a steady stream of client reviews, remains one of the highest-return investments available to a workers' compensation practice. Local map listings appear prominently above traditional organic results for searches like \"workers comp lawyer near me,\" and injured workers searching from a phone in a break room or waiting room tend to click a nearby, well-reviewed listing before scrolling further down the page." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Build Content Around the Questions Injured Workers Actually Ask" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Organic search traffic compounds over time in a way paid channels don't, and workers' compensation searches skew heavily toward specific questions: whether a claim can be filed after being fired, what happens if an employer disputes an injury, or whether a worker can choose their own doctor. Content built directly around these real questions, rather than a generic overview of workers' compensation law, tends to rank better and converts at a higher rate because it matches exactly what the searcher typed in." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Use Search Ads to Fill the Gaps SEO Can't Cover Immediately" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Organic rankings take months to build, which is where search ads earn their place in a workers' compensation legal marketing strategy. Bidding on high-intent terms lets a firm appear at the top of results immediately while its organic content matures in the background. The keywords that convert best for workers' comp tend to be narrower and more urgent than broad personal injury terms, and firms that build separate ad groups around specific scenarios, such as a denied claim or a workplace injury involving a specific type of accident, typically see a lower cost per lead than firms running one broad campaign." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Don't Overlook Video Advertising" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "YouTube advertising and short-form video ads reach injured workers earlier in their search process, often before they've typed a single query into Google, and video allows an attorney to build trust in a way static text and images can't match. A short, direct video explaining what to do after a workplace injury, paired with clear contact information, performs particularly well because it addresses the anxiety and confusion many injured workers feel in the days immediately following an incident." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Channels Worth Testing for Workers' Comp Marketing" },
      { type: "list", items: [
        "Local map listings and Google Business Profile optimization for near-me searches.",
        "Question-based blog and FAQ content targeting specific workers' comp scenarios.",
        "Search ads on high-intent, narrow keyword phrases rather than broad terms.",
        "YouTube and short-form video advertising to build early trust.",
        "Retargeting campaigns for visitors who didn't convert on their first site visit.",
      ] },
      { type: "heading", text: "Retarget Visitors Who Don't Convert on the First Visit" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Most visitors who land on a firm's website after clicking a search ad or an organic result will not fill out a form or call on that first visit, especially if they're still weighing whether their situation even qualifies for a claim. A retargeting campaign that follows those visitors across other websites and social platforms with a modest, relevant ad keeps the firm visible during that decision window, and it typically costs far less per impression than the original click that brought them to the site in the first place. Firms that skip retargeting are effectively paying once to reach a prospect and then letting that investment disappear the moment the browser tab closes." },
      { type: "heading", text: "Measuring What's Actually Working" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Because workers' compensation leads often take longer to convert than other personal injury matters, tracking cost per lead alone can be misleading. Firms that also track cost per signed case, broken down by channel, get a much clearer picture of which marketing dollars are actually producing revenue rather than simply producing form submissions that never turn into retained clients. A channel that looks expensive on a cost-per-lead basis can still be the most profitable one once the higher conversion rate of its leads is factored into the comparison, which is why judging channels on lead cost alone so often leads firms to the wrong conclusion." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "No single channel reliably carries a workers' compensation legal marketing strategy on its own. The firms that consistently generate workers compensation leads tend to be the ones running local SEO, targeted content, search ads, and video together, using each channel to reinforce the others rather than treating them as separate, competing budgets." },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "What's the fastest way to start generating workers' comp leads?", a: "Search ads produce results immediately, while local SEO and content marketing build momentum over months. Most firms run both in parallel so paid traffic bridges the gap until organic rankings mature." },
      { q: "How important are Google reviews for workers' comp marketing?", a: "Very important. Map listings weigh review volume and recency heavily, and injured workers researching an attorney frequently read several reviews before deciding who to call." },
      { q: "Should firms bid on broad or narrow keywords for workers' comp?", a: "Narrow, scenario-specific keywords generally convert better and cost less per lead than broad terms, since they match searchers who are further along in deciding to contact an attorney." },
      { q: "Does video advertising work for workers' compensation cases?", a: "Yes, particularly for building trust early in the search process. A clear, direct video explaining next steps after a workplace injury often outperforms static ads on engagement." },
      { q: "Is retargeting worth the extra budget for a workers' comp campaign?", a: "Usually yes. Retargeting impressions cost a fraction of the original click and keep the firm visible while an injured worker is still deciding whether to pursue a claim." },
    ],
